== ABI v1 obligation 12.5: the concurrency suite ==
-std=201703  impl=target  guard=on  linkage=shared  threads=4  rounds=6
plants: AK_CONC_PAD=1  AK_CONC_GLOBAL=0   <-- a REFUSED design from ABI v1 section 6, built on purpose
hardware_concurrency=4

-- the reference bytes, anchored to manifest.json --
   P1.1  858 B
   P1.3  605 B
   P1.2  218121 B
   P2.2  540422 B

-- T0: does this shape set have a history surface at all? --
   P1.1 writes 0 of 35 length-prefix sites
   P1.3 writes 0 of 35 length-prefix sites
   P1.2 writes 0 of 35 length-prefix sites
   P2.2 writes 0 of 35 length-prefix sites
   (AK_CONC_PAD: this probe reads `resize_prefix`, which the plant removes on
    the narrowing side, so it under-reports here. The surface is the shipped
    build's, above.)

-- T1: the encoder's output does not depend on its own history --
   Sequential, one thread. The learned width survives a reset by design, so
   this asks whether what survives can be seen on the wire. Needs two shapes.

  FAIL  native: P1.3 after P1.1 is byte-identical to P1.3 on a virgin encoder
  FAIL  native: P1.3 after P1.2 is byte-identical to P1.3 on a virgin encoder
   12 pairs checked on two arms.

-- T2 and T3: the same work, run in sequence and then together --
   4 threads x 6 rounds x 4 shapes, every encode memcmp'd against the
   reference. T2 joins each thread before starting the next, so the two
   differ only by overlap: a defect that shows in both is not a concurrency
   defect, and that distinction is why 12.5 asks for both.

  T2 threads in sequence                   96 encodes   native 23  ffi 0  ffi-hosttc 0  roundtrip 23  spurious-err 0
  FAIL  T2: every encode in sequence matched the reference
  T3 threads together                      96 encodes   native 23  ffi 0  ffi-hosttc 0  roundtrip 23  spurious-err 0
  FAIL  T3: every encode in parallel matched the reference

-- T4: a failure on one thread is not visible on another --
   Half the threads feed truncated input and must fail; half feed the
   reference and must succeed with err == 0. Concurrently, 24 rounds.

  FAIL  every good decode produced the reference bytes
   truncated-but-accepted 0, good-but-wrong 12, error leaked across a context 0

-- T5: two threads with different histories encode the same message --
   Every ordered pair: thread A encodes the prior and then the target,
   thread B encodes the target alone. Both must produce the reference AND
   agree with each other -- agreement is the half that a manifest comparison
   alone would miss if both drifted the same way, which is exactly what a
   process-global table does.

   P1.1 then P1.3: disagreed 12, wrong 12 (of 12 rounds)
   P1.2 then P1.3: disagreed 12, wrong 12 (of 12 rounds)
  FAIL  no ordered pair made two threads disagree
  FAIL  no ordered pair made a thread diverge from the reference
   12 ordered pairs x 12 rounds: disagreements 24, wrong against the reference 24

-- T6: one shape against two, same threads, same rounds --
   12.5: "a suite with one shape reports zero wrong bytes with a
   per-thread-state defect present and absent alike; two shapes find it in
   twenty encodes out of twenty." That is a claim, so it is measured. On the
   shipped design every row below is zero; the three planted builds are where
   the row that matters is not.

  one shape  (P1.1 alone)        24 encodes,    0 wrong  (native 0, ffi 0, hosttc 0, roundtrip 0)
  one shape  (P1.2 alone)        24 encodes,    0 wrong  (native 0, ffi 0, hosttc 0, roundtrip 0)
  two shapes (P1.1 + P1.2)       48 encodes,   44 wrong  (native 22, ffi 0, hosttc 0, roundtrip 22)
  four shapes (P1.x + P2.x)      96 encodes,   46 wrong  (native 23, ffi 0, hosttc 0, roundtrip 23)


